/* trolley.lps — Kowalski, ch. 12 §12.2–12.3: the runaway trolley, and the
 * computational case for moral constraints.
 *
 * The book's argument is not about which answer is right. It is that a moral
 * *constraint* does a different computational job from a moral *preference*:
 * a preference is weighed against other things, and a constraint removes the
 * option from consideration entirely — which is why an agent with constraints
 * decides faster and can say what it refused.
 *
 * LE could express the beliefs (examples/moreExamples/rkBook/trolley_problem.le
 * does). What it could not express is the constraint, and the cycle in which
 * the constraint bites before the action happens.
 */

maxTime(8).

fluents  trolley_on(_), people_on(_), alive(_), dead(_).
events   runaway, notices.
actions  divert, push(_), do_nothing.

initially trolley_on(main), people_on(main), people_on(side),
	  alive(five), alive(one), alive(bystander).

divert      updates Old to side in trolley_on(Old).
push(_)     updates Old to side in trolley_on(Old).

%  What the trolley does to whoever is on its track.
divert      terminates alive(one).
divert      initiates dead(one).
do_nothing  terminates alive(five).
do_nothing  initiates dead(five).
push(B)     terminates alive(B).
push(B)     initiates dead(B).

/* The agent's goal is an *outcome* — get the trolley off the main line — and
   there are two ways to reach it. They are written with the forbidden one
   first, deliberately: the engine tries pushing, the constraint refuses, and
   it backtracks to diverting. That is the book's argument made operational,
   and it is why `why_not` has something to say afterwards.
*/
save_the_five from T1 to T2 if push(bystander) from T1 to T2.
save_the_five from T1 to T2 if divert from T1 to T2.

if   trolley_on(main) at T1, alive(five) at T1
then save_the_five from T1 to T2.

/* §12.3's constraint. Diverting kills one as a *side effect*; pushing the
   bystander kills them as the *means*. The book's point is that the second is
   what a moral constraint forbids, and that forbidding it is cheap — the
   option never enters the search.
*/
false push(_).

%  …and the explanation is available afterwards:
%    ./lps explain examples/rkbook/trolley.lps --ask "why_not(happened(push(bystander)), 2)"
%
%  [blocked_by_denial] a denial blocked push(bystander) — false [happens(push(bystander),1,2)]

observe runaway from 2 to 3.
